atonic philosophers like Porphyry, indicating her lasting scholarly impact."}, {"sourceSlug": "aristoxenus", "sourceName": "Aristoxenus", "verb": "FRAMES", "targetSlug": "ptolemais-of-cyrene", "targetName": "Ptolemais of Cyrene", "context": "Ptolemais's work often framed its arguments in contrast to the empirical musical theories of Aristoxenus, highlighting their distinct methodological approaches."}], "places": [{"name": "Cyrene, Libya", "role": "Center of scholarship"}, {"name": "Alexandria, Egypt", "role": "Hellenistic intellectual hub"}, {"name": "Athens, Greece", "role": "Philosophical influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ptolemais of Cyrene", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ptolemais-of-cyrene"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q525814", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ptolemais_of_Cyrene"], "tags": ["Philosophers & Thinkers", "doctrine-development", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Ptolemais of Cyrene is historically significant as the only known female music theorist from antiquity, whose work preserved and transmitted Pythagorean musical principles. Her unique position offers rare insight into female intellectual contributions in a male-dominated scholarly world, influencing later theorists and philosophers.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-22T08:16:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45001656010", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Ptolemais of Cyrene was a music theorist, author of Pythagorean Principles of Music. She lived perhaps in the 3rd century BC, and "certainly not after the first century AD." She is the only known female music theorist of antiquity.", "newValue": "Ptolemais of Cyrene, an enigmatic figure of Hellenistic antiquity, stands as the sole known female music theorist from the ancient world. Flourishing perhaps in the 3rd century BCE, though certainly no later than the 1st century CE, her work, *Pythagorean Principles of Music* (Πυθαγορικὴ τῆς μουσικῆς στοιχειώσις), critically engaged with the mathematical and philosophical underpinnings of music. Her scholarship, preserved primarily through later citations by figures like Porphyry, places her within the vibrant intellectual tradition of Cyrene, a major Greek city in ancient Libya renowned for its philosophical and scientific contributions.

Her primary contribution was a comprehensive exposition of Pythagorean music theory, meticulously outlining the principles of harmonic ratios, intervals, and scales, grounding musical consonance and dissonance in rigorous mathematical relationships. Ptolemais distinguished between the empirical, sensation-based approach of Aristoxenus and the rational, number-based system championed by Pythagoras and his followers, advocating for the latter's intellectual rigor. Her treatise likely served as a foundational text for understanding the mathematical structure of music, a core component of ancient Greek education and philosophy.

